---
import Site from '../../layouts/Site.astro'
import { Code } from 'astro:components'
import { CopyCommand } from '../../components/CopyCommand'
import { SITE_HOST, installCommand } from '../../data/site'

const TOC = [
  { id: 'prerequisites', label: 'Prerequisites' },
  { id: 'stylesheet', label: 'The stylesheet' },
  { id: 'quick-start', label: 'Quick start' },
  { id: 'forms', label: 'Forms' },
  { id: 'theming', label: 'Theming' },
  { id: 'dark-mode', label: 'Dark mode' },
  { id: 'conventions', label: 'Conventions' },
  { id: 'accessibility', label: 'Accessibility' },
  { id: 'blocks-vs-templates', label: 'Blocks vs. templates' },
]
const TOC_TONES = ['purple', 'mint', 'blue', 'pink', 'yellow', 'orange', 'purple', 'mint', 'blue']
---

<Site title="Getting started" description="Everything you need before the first install: prerequisites, the theme file, dark mode, and the conventions every 1st-Pouf component follows." wide>
  <section class="docs-hero">
    <div class="docs-hero__copy">
      <span class="sticker" style="background:var(--mint); align-self:flex-start; transform:rotate(-2deg);">You own the source</span>
      <h1 class="display">Getting started</h1>
      <p class="docs-hero__lead">
        1st-Pouf is distributed as a shadcn-style registry. You install components into your own codebase and own the source — there is no runtime dependency to lock, nothing to update, nothing to break.
      </p>
      <p class="docs-hero__detail">
        This page covers what you need before the first <code>add</code>, the two-layer token system, dark mode, and the conventions every component follows.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-hero__path" aria-label="Guide overview">
      <a href="#prerequisites" style="--step-tone:var(--purple);">
        <span>01</span>
        <strong>Prepare</strong>
      </a>
      <a href="#quick-start" style="--step-tone:var(--mint);">
        <span>02</span>
        <strong>Install</strong>
      </a>
      <a href="#theming" style="--step-tone:var(--pink);">
        <span>03</span>
        <strong>Make it yours</strong>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <details class="docs-mobile-toc">
    <summary>Jump to a section</summary>
    <nav aria-label="On this page">
      {TOC.map((t, index) => (
        <a class="docs-toc-link" href={`#${t.id}`}>
          <span style={`background:var(--${TOC_TONES[index]});`}>{index + 1}</span>
          {t.label}
        </a>
      ))}
    </nav>
  </details>

  <div class="docs-grid">
    <aside class="docs-sidebar">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <nav aria-label="On this page">
        {TOC.map((t, index) => (
          <a class="docs-toc-link" href={`#${t.id}`}>
            <span style={`background:var(--${TOC_TONES[index]});`}>{index + 1}</span>
            {t.label}
          </a>
        ))}
      </nav>
    </aside>

    <article class="docs-content">
      <section id="prerequisites" class="docs-section" style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:12px;">
        <h2 style="font-size:24px; font-weight:900; margin:16px 0 0;">1. Prerequisites</h2>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">Before your first <code>add</code>, you need three things in place:</p>
        <ul style="margin:0; padding-left:22px; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:10px; font-weight:700;">
          <li><strong>A React 18 or 19 app.</strong> Every published registry file is compatibility-tested with React 18.3.1, while 1st-Pouf's development, gallery, and documentation apps run on React 19. Vite, Next, Astro with the React integration — use whatever you already run. React 17 and older are not supported because the animation-enabled components use Framer Motion 11, whose supported range starts at React 18.</li>
          <li><strong>Tailwind CSS v4.</strong> 1st-Pouf's stylesheet is CSS-first — no <code>tailwind.config.js</code>, just <code>@theme</code> and <code>@import</code> in a <code>.css</code> file. You still need Tailwind's v4 build plugin wired into your bundler: <code>@tailwindcss/vite</code> for a Vite-based app (that's what {SITE_HOST} itself runs — an Astro site on Vite), or <code>@tailwindcss/postcss</code> for a plain PostCSS pipeline.</li>
          <li><strong>The shadcn CLI, initialized.</strong> Run <code>init</code> once to create a <code>components.json</code>. 1st-Pouf doesn't need special aliases: every file in the registry declares an explicit target path (<code>components/pouf/…</code>), so it lands in the same place no matter how your aliases are configured.</li>
        </ul>
        <CopyCommand command="npx shadcn@latest init" />
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          Everything past that rides along automatically. Radix UI primitives, <code>class-variance-authority</code>, <code>clsx</code>, and — for a handful of components — <code>framer-motion</code> are declared as npm dependencies on the individual registry items that need them, so the CLI installs exactly what a given component uses the moment you <code>add</code> it. Nothing to pre-install by hand.
        </p>
      </section>

      <hr style="border:none; height:2px; background:var(--purple); opacity:0.2; margin:4px 0;" />

      <section id="stylesheet" class="docs-section" style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:12px;">
        <h2 style="font-size:24px; font-weight:900; margin:16px 0 0;">2. The stylesheet</h2>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          The <code>base</code> registry item ships one file — <code>pouf.css</code> — the theme: every design token, the cushion box-shadow recipes, keyframes, and the chrome for multi-part components (dialogs, menus, the mobile sheet). Every other component depends on it, so it always comes along, but you can install it explicitly:
        </p>
        <CopyCommand command={installCommand('base')} />
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">It lands at <code>components/pouf/pouf.css</code>. Import it once, at your app root, alongside the font:</p>
        <Code code={`import '@fontsource-variable/nunito'
import './components/pouf/pouf.css'`} lang="ts" theme="material-theme-palenight" />
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          Two details worth knowing before you go looking for a Tailwind entry file elsewhere: <code>pouf.css</code> <em>is</em> your Tailwind entry. Its first line is <code>@import 'tailwindcss'</code> — importing <code>pouf.css</code> is what pulls Tailwind itself into your build. Don't add a second <code>@import 'tailwindcss'</code> anywhere.
        </p>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          Its second line is <code>@source './'</code> — Tailwind v4's automatic content scanner normally only looks at your own app's files. This directive tells it to also scan everything next to <code>pouf.css</code> itself, which is where the components you install actually live. Skip it (say, by copying only pieces of the file) and Tailwind never sees the utility classes baked into the <code>.tsx</code> sources you just installed — every cushion renders unstyled.
        </p>
      </section>

      <hr style="border:none; height:2px; background:var(--purple); opacity:0.2; margin:4px 0;" />

      <section id="quick-start" class="docs-section" style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:12px;">
        <h2 style="font-size:24px; font-weight:900; margin:16px 0 0;">3. Quick start</h2>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">With the base in place, add a component:</p>
        <CopyCommand command={installCommand('button')} />
        <Code code={`import { Button } from './components/pouf/Button'

export function Example() {
  return <Button tone="mint">Save</Button>
}`} lang="tsx" theme="material-theme-palenight" />
      </section>

      <hr style="border:none; height:2px; background:var(--purple); opacity:0.2; margin:4px 0;" />

      <section id="forms" class="docs-section" style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:12px;">
        <h2 style="font-size:24px; font-weight:900; margin:16px 0 0;">4. Forms</h2>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          1st-Pouf does not choose a form library for you. Its controls expose controlled <code>value</code>/<code>onChange</code> APIs, and text fields additionally forward <code>ref</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>onBlur</code>, autocomplete, and native input attributes. Use plain React state, native forms, Formik, or anything else.
        </p>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          The examples use <a href="https://react-hook-form.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color:var(--ink);">React Hook Form</a>. Its <code>Controller</code> maps directly to every controlled 1st-Pouf form component while keeping validation and submission in the example rather than the design system.
        </p>
        <CopyCommand command="npm install react-hook-form" />
        <Code code={`import { Controller, useForm } from 'react-hook-form'
import { Button } from './components/pouf/Button'
import { Checkbox } from './components/pouf/checkbox'
import { Combobox, Select, Switch } from './components/pouf/controls'
import { Field, Input, Textarea } from './components/pouf/Input'
import { NumberInput } from './components/pouf/NumberInput'
import { RadioGroup } from './components/pouf/radiogroup'
import { Segmented } from './components/pouf/Segmented'
import { Slider } from './components/pouf/slider'
import { ToggleGroup } from './components/pouf/toggle'

type FormValues = {
  email: string
  bio: string
  age: string
  fruit: string
  timezone: string
  updates: boolean
  publicProfile: boolean
  plan: string
  volume: number
  view: 'grid' | 'list'
  topics: string[]
}

const defaults: FormValues = {
  email: '',
  bio: '',
  age: '18',
  fruit: '',
  timezone: '',
  updates: true,
  publicProfile: false,
  plan: 'solo',
  volume: 60,
  view: 'grid',
  topics: [],
}

export function ProfileForm() {
  const {
    control,
    handleSubmit,
    formState: { errors, isSubmitting },
  } = useForm<FormValues>({ defaultValues: defaults })

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(console.log)} noValidate>
      <Field label="Email" error={errors.email?.message}>
        {(id, describedBy) => (
          <Controller
            name="email"
            control={control}
            rules={{ required: 'Email is required.' }}
            render={({ field }) => (
              <Input
                {...field}
                id={id}
                describedBy={describedBy}
                type="email"
                autoComplete="email"
                invalid={!!errors.email}
              />
            )}
          />
        )}
      </Field>

      <Field label="Bio">
        {(id, describedBy) => (
          <Controller
            name="bio"
            control={control}
            render={({ field }) => (
              <Textarea {...field} id={id} describedBy={describedBy} />
            )}
          />
        )}
      </Field>

      <Controller
        name="age"
        control={control}
        render={({ field }) => <NumberInput value={field.value} onChange={field.onChange} min={0} label="Age" />}
      />
      <Controller
        name="fruit"
        control={control}
        render={({ field }) => (
          <Select
            value={field.value}
            onChange={field.onChange}
            label="Fruit"
            options={[{ value: 'apple', label: 'Apple' }, { value: 'pear', label: 'Pear' }]}
          />
        )}
      />
      <Controller
        name="timezone"
        control={control}
        render={({ field }) => (
          <Combobox
            value={field.value}
            onChange={field.onChange}
            label="Timezone"
            options={['Europe/London', 'Asia/Tokyo']}
          />
        )}
      />
      <Controller
        name="updates"
        control={control}
        render={({ field }) => <Checkbox checked={field.value} onChange={field.onChange} label="Email updates" />}
      />
      <Controller
        name="publicProfile"
        control={control}
        render={({ field }) => <Switch checked={field.value} onChange={field.onChange} label="Public profile" />}
      />
      <Controller
        name="plan"
        control={control}
        render={({ field }) => (
          <RadioGroup
            value={field.value}
            onChange={field.onChange}
            label="Plan"
            options={[{ value: 'solo', label: 'Solo' }, { value: 'team', label: 'Team' }]}
          />
        )}
      />
      <Controller
        name="volume"
        control={control}
        render={({ field }) => <Slider value={[field.value]} onChange={(value) => field.onChange(value[0])} label="Volume" />}
      />
      <Controller
        name="view"
        control={control}
        render={({ field }) => (
          <Segmented
            value={field.value}
            onChange={field.onChange}
            label="View"
            options={[{ value: 'grid', label: 'Grid' }, { value: 'list', label: 'List' }]}
          />
        )}
      />
      <Controller
        name="topics"
        control={control}
        render={({ field }) => (
          <ToggleGroup
            value={field.value}
            onChange={field.onChange}
            label="Topics"
            options={[{ value: 'design', label: 'Design' }, { value: 'code', label: 'Code' }]}
          />
        )}
      />

      <Button type="submit" loading={isSubmitting}>Save profile</Button>
    </form>
  )
}`} lang="tsx" theme="material-theme-palenight" />
      </section>

      <hr style="border:none; height:2px; background:var(--purple); opacity:0.2; margin:4px 0;" />

      <section id="theming" class="docs-section" style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:12px;">
        <h2 style="font-size:24px; font-weight:900; margin:16px 0 0;">5. Theming</h2>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          Every token in <code>pouf.css</code> is a plain CSS custom property, in two layers.
        </p>
        <h3 style="font-size:17px; font-weight:900; margin:8px 0 0;"><code>@theme</code> — the Tailwind-facing layer</h3>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          This is what Tailwind reads to generate utility classes (<code>bg-purple</code>, <code>rounded-card</code>, …). It's the palette and radii, verbatim from the reference design:
        </p>
        <Code code={`@theme {
  --color-bg: #f0e9ff;
  --color-ink: #3a2e5c;
  --color-purple: #c9a8ff;
  --color-mint: #a8f0d0;
  /* …pink, blue, yellow, orange, muted, surface */

  --radius-card: 32px;
  --radius-control: 20px;
  --radius-blob: 24px;
}`} lang="css" theme="material-theme-palenight" />
        <h3 style="font-size:17px; font-weight:900; margin:8px 0 0;"><code>:root</code> — the layer components actually speak</h3>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          Short aliases that resolve to the same values (<code>--bg</code>, <code>--ink</code>, <code>--surface</code>, <code>--purple</code>, …), a spacing scale (<code>--s1</code> through <code>--s8</code>), and the clay recipe itself — the layered box-shadows every cushion is built from: <code>--pouf-card</code>, <code>--pouf-control</code>, <code>--pouf-field</code>, <code>--pouf-row</code> (each with hover/active/focus siblings). Components read these names, not <code>@theme</code>'s, and not a hard-coded colour anywhere.
        </p>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          <strong>To re-theme the palette</strong>, override the <code>@theme</code> values in your own stylesheet, after importing <code>pouf.css</code>. Because <code>:root</code>'s aliases are written as <code>var(--color-purple)</code> — a live reference, not a copied value — one change reaches everything: the <code>:root</code> aliases, the clay recipe, and the handful of components that use a compiled Tailwind utility (like <code>bg-purple</code>) directly.
        </p>
        <Code code={`/* your own stylesheet, imported after pouf.css */
@theme {
  --color-purple: #7c5cff;
  --radius-card: 20px;
}`} lang="css" theme="material-theme-palenight" />
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          <strong>To remap what a tone means</strong> rather than its hue, override the semantic alias directly — screens speak intent (<code>--up</code>, <code>--down</code>, <code>--warn</code>, <code>--info</code>, <code>--idle</code>), never colour, so a re-theme of meaning happens in exactly one place:
        </p>
        <Code code={`:root {
  --down: var(--orange); /* was --pink */
}`} lang="css" theme="material-theme-palenight" />
      </section>

      <hr style="border:none; height:2px; background:var(--purple); opacity:0.2; margin:4px 0;" />

      <section id="dark-mode" class="docs-section" style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:12px;">
        <h2 style="font-size:24px; font-weight:900; margin:16px 0 0;">6. Dark mode</h2>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          Dark is opt-in on the document, not a media query: set <code>data-theme="dark"</code> or the conventional shadcn <code>class="dark"</code> on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> and every dark token in <code>pouf.css</code> takes over. Leave both off (or use their light equivalents) and the default palette remains.
        </p>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          There is deliberately no <code>@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)</code> rule in the stylesheet. Reading the OS preference in CSS would make the theme a property of the visitor's machine rather than of the document — every screenshot test would become environment-dependent, and the choice would be taken away from your app, which usually already owns a theme toggle. Deciding from the OS preference is a one-line job for whoever mounts the app, done once, in script — not baked into the library.
        </p>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">Here's the standard no-flash version — synchronous and inline in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, so it runs before first paint (a deferred script, or a React effect, both run after the browser has already painted the wrong theme):</p>
        <Code code={`<script>
  (function () {
    var media = window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)')
    try {
      var saved = localStorage.getItem('theme')
      var theme =
        saved === 'light' || saved === 'dark'
          ? saved
          : media.matches
            ? 'dark'
            : 'light'
      document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', theme)
    } catch (e) {
      document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', media.matches ? 'dark' : 'light')
    }
  })()
</script>`} lang="html" theme="material-theme-palenight" />
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          Toggling later is one line — <code>document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', next)</code> — plus whatever persistence you want (this site's own toggle writes the choice to <code>localStorage</code> so it survives a reload).
        </p>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          One token needs a closer look across the swap: <code>--on-accent</code> — the ink colour that sits <em>on</em> a pastel accent fill (a solid Button, a filled Badge, an active NavLink, a Blob). The accents themselves never change between themes — they're the brand — while <code>--ink</code> flips from near-black to near-white for the page. Collapse the two and dark mode puts near-white text on a fill that's still pastel purple, which fails contrast outright. <code>--on-accent</code> is pinned separately for dark (it stays dark, because the fill it sits on stays pastel); in light mode it resolves to exactly <code>--ink</code>, so nothing about the light theme changes.
        </p>
      </section>

      <hr style="border:none; height:2px; background:var(--purple); opacity:0.2; margin:4px 0;" />

      <section id="conventions" class="docs-section" style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:12px;">
        <h2 style="font-size:24px; font-weight:900; margin:16px 0 0;">7. Conventions</h2>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          No component takes a <code>className</code> or <code>style</code> prop. Appearance is chosen with variants — <code>tone</code>, <code>size</code>, <code>variant</code> — so your UI can't quietly drift off-system one utility class at a time. Tones are <code>purple, pink, blue, mint, yellow, orange</code>, plus five semantic aliases that speak intent instead of colour: <code>up, down, warn, info, idle</code> (see Theming above for what they map to, and how to remap them).
        </p>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          When you genuinely need an escape hatch that no variant covers, you own the file — edit it. That is the whole trade 1st-Pouf makes: a strong default that holds, with the source in your hands when it shouldn't.
        </p>
      </section>

      <hr style="border:none; height:2px; background:var(--purple); opacity:0.2; margin:4px 0;" />

      <section id="accessibility" class="docs-section" style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:12px;">
        <h2 style="font-size:24px; font-weight:900; margin:16px 0 0;">8. Accessibility</h2>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          Overlay behaviour — focus trapping, keyboard nav, ARIA state — comes from Radix, for free. Two conventions worth knowing before you build a form or a toolbar:
        </p>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          <strong>Field</strong> wires a real <code>&lt;label for&gt;</code>, hint text, and an error message together, and hands the ids back to you rather than making you guess them. It takes a render-prop child — a function, not plain JSX — because the same wrapper serves Input, Select, and Switch without duplicating the <code>aria-describedby</code> plumbing per control:
        </p>
        <Code code={`<Field label="Email" hint="We'll never share it.">
  {(id, describedBy) => (
    <Input id={id} describedBy={describedBy} value={email} onChange={setEmail} />
  )}
</Field>`} lang="tsx" theme="material-theme-palenight" />
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          <strong>Icon-only controls need a <code>label</code> prop.</strong> A Button (or a NumberInput stepper, a Checkbox, a chart legend, …) with only a glyph inside has no accessible name unless you supply one — it renders as <code>aria-label</code>:
        </p>
        <Code code={`<Button label="Delete row" size="sm" variant="quiet">
  <Icon name="remove" />
</Button>`} lang="tsx" theme="material-theme-palenight" />
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          Don't also pass a <code>label</code> to the <code>Icon</code> in that case — <code>Icon</code>'s own <code>label</code> exists for when the glyph is the <em>sole</em> carrier of meaning with nothing else naming it. Set both and a screen reader announces the description twice.
        </p>
      </section>

      <hr style="border:none; height:2px; background:var(--purple); opacity:0.2; margin:4px 0;" />

      <section id="blocks-vs-templates" class="docs-section" style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:12px;">
        <h2 style="font-size:24px; font-weight:900; margin:16px 0 0;">9. Blocks vs. templates</h2>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          Past a single component, 1st-Pouf ships two tiers of copy-pasteable composition. Both install exactly like a component — a registry item that lands with its own dependencies — but they promise different things:
        </p>
        <ul style="margin:0; padding-left:22px; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:10px; font-weight:700;">
          <li><strong>Blocks are sections.</strong> A pricing table, a login card, a music player — you drop one into a page you already have.</li>
          <li><strong>Templates are screens.</strong> Global chrome (an app shell with navigation, or a marketing page with a navbar and footer) plus the content inside it — you ship one as a route.</li>
        </ul>
        <CopyCommand command={installCommand('dashboard')} />
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          Browse every block on <a href="/blocks" style="color:var(--ink);">/blocks</a>, and every template on <a href="/examples" style="color:var(--ink);">/examples</a>.
        </p>
        <p style="margin:0; font-weight:700;">
          Templates are complete routes, not oversized snippets. They include responsive navigation, realistic content, working core interactions, and meaningful states. Install one, replace the sample data, then connect your router, persistence, and service adapters.
        </p>
      </section>

      <p style="margin:16px 0 0; font-weight:800;">Next: browse <a href="/components" style="color:var(--ink);">every component</a> live on one page — copy an install command, or read the source.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Site>

<style>
  .docs-hero {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.5fr) minmax(250px, 0.7fr);
    gap: clamp(28px, 5vw, 64px);
    align-items: end;
    margin: 8px 0 42px;
    padding: clamp(28px, 5vw, 58px);
    overflow: hidden;
    border-radius: 36px;
    background:
      radial-gradient(circle at 92% 12%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--pink) 72%, transparent) 0 76px, transparent 77px),
      radial-gradient(circle at 76% 115%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--mint) 72%, transparent) 0 110px, transparent 111px),
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--surface) 90%, transparent);
    box-shadow: var(--pouf-card);
  }
  .docs-hero::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    z-index: -1;
    width: 150px;
    height: 150px;
    top: -78px;
    right: 25%;
    border-radius: 44% 56% 58% 42%;
